{
  "semantics": {
    "attributes": {
      "final": {},
      "header": {}
    },
    "blocks": [
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "This section describes how Spring handles the crucial pointcut concept."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "preamble",
        "id": null,
        "outline": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Spring’s pointcut model enables pointcut reuse independent of advice types. You can target different advice with the same pointcut."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "org.springframework.aop.Pointcut"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " interface is the central interface, used to target advice to particular classes and methods. The complete interface follows:"
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "listing",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "public interface Pointcut {\n\n\tClassFilter getClassFilter();\n\n\tMethodMatcher getMethodMatcher();\n}"
              }
            ],
            "language": "java",
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Splitting the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Pointcut"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " interface into two parts allows reuse of class and method matching parts and fine-grained composition operations (such as performing a “union” with another method matcher)."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ClassFilter"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " interface is used to restrict the pointcut to a given set of target classes. If the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "matches()"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method always returns true, all target classes are matched. The following listing shows the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ClassFilter"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " interface definition:"
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "listing",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "public interface ClassFilter {\n\n\tboolean matches(Class clazz);\n}"
              }
            ],
            "language": "java",
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "MethodMatcher"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " interface is normally more important. The complete interface follows:"
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "listing",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "public interface MethodMatcher {\n\n\tboolean matches(Method m, Class<?> targetClass);\n\n\tboolean isRuntime();\n\n\tboolean matches(Method m, Class<?> targetClass, Object... args);\n}"
              }
            ],
            "language": "java",
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "The "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "matches(Method, Class)"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method is used to test whether this pointcut ever matches a given method on a target class. This evaluation can be performed when an AOP proxy is created to avoid the need for a test on every method invocation. If the two-argument "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "matches"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method returns "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "true"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for a given method, and the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "isRuntime()"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method for the MethodMatcher returns "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "true"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ", the three-argument matches method is invoked on every method invocation. This lets a pointcut look at the arguments passed to the method invocation immediately before the target advice starts."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Most "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "MethodMatcher"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " implementations are static, meaning that their "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "isRuntime()"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method returns "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "false"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ". In this case, the three-argument "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "matches"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " method is never invoked."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "admonition",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "If possible, try to make pointcuts static, allowing the AOP framework to cache the results of pointcut evaluation when an AOP proxy is created."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null,
            "type": "tip"
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "aop-api-concepts",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Concepts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Spring supports operations (notably, union and intersection) on pointcuts."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Union means the methods that either pointcut matches. Intersection means the methods that both pointcuts match. Union is usually more useful. You can compose pointcuts by using the static methods in the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "org.springframework.aop.support.Pointcuts"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " class or by using the "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "ComposablePointcut"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " class in the same package. However, using AspectJ pointcut expressions is usually a simpler approach."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "aop-api-pointcut-ops",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Operations on Pointcuts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Since 2.0, the most important type of pointcut used by Spring is "
              },
              {
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "org.springframework.aop.aspectj.AspectJExpressionPointcut"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "monospace",
                "roles": []
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": ". This is a pointcut that uses an AspectJ-supplied library to parse an AspectJ pointcut expression string."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "See the "
              },
              {
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "previous chapter"
                  }
                ],
                "kind": "xref",
                "target": "core/aop.html"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": " for a discussion of supported AspectJ pointcut primitives."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-aspectj",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "AspectJ Expression Pointcuts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Spring provides several convenient pointcut implementations. You can use some of them directly; others are intended to be subclassed in application-specific pointcuts."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
          {
            "blocks": [
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Static pointcuts are based on the method and the target class and cannot take into account the method’s arguments. Static pointcuts suffice — and are best — for most usages. Spring can evaluate a static pointcut only once, when a method is first invoked. After that, there is no need to evaluate the pointcut again with each method invocation."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              },
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "The rest of this section describes some of the static pointcut implementations that are included with Spring."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              },
              {
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "One obvious way to specify static pointcuts is regular expressions. Several AOP frameworks besides Spring make this possible. "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "org.springframework.aop.support.JdkRegexpMethodPointcut"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " is a generic regular expression pointcut that uses the regular expression support in the JDK."
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "With the "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "JdkRegexpMethodPointcut"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " class, you can provide a list of pattern strings. If any of these is a match, the pointcut evaluates to "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "true"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": ". (As a consequence, the resulting pointcut is effectively the union of the specified patterns.)"
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "The following example shows how to use "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "JdkRegexpMethodPointcut"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": ":"
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "include-code::./JdkRegexpConfiguration[tag=snippet,indent=0]"
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "Spring provides a convenience class named "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "RegexpMethodPointcutAdvisor"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": ", which lets us also reference an "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "Advice"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " (remember that an "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "Advice"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " can be an interceptor, before advice, throws advice, and others). Behind the scenes, Spring uses a "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "JdkRegexpMethodPointcut"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": ". Using "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "RegexpMethodPointcutAdvisor"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " simplifies wiring, as the one bean encapsulates both pointcut and advice, as the following example shows:"
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "include-code::./RegexpConfiguration[tag=snippet,indent=0]"
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "You can use "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "RegexpMethodPointcutAdvisor"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " with any "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "Advice"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " type."
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  }
                ],
                "context": "section",
                "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-regex",
                "level": 3,
                "roles": [],
                "title": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Regular Expression Pointcuts"
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "An important type of static pointcut is a metadata-driven pointcut. This uses the values of metadata attributes (typically, source-level metadata)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  }
                ],
                "context": "section",
                "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-attribute-driven",
                "level": 3,
                "roles": [],
                "title": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Attribute-driven Pointcuts"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "context": "section",
            "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-static",
            "level": 2,
            "roles": [],
            "title": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Static Pointcuts"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "blocks": [
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Dynamic pointcuts are costlier to evaluate than static pointcuts. They take into account method arguments as well as static information. This means that they must be evaluated with every method invocation and that the result cannot be cached, as arguments will vary."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              },
              {
                "context": "paragraph",
                "id": null,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "The main example is the "
                  },
                  {
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "control flow"
                      }
                    ],
                    "kind": "monospace",
                    "roles": []
                  },
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": " pointcut."
                  }
                ],
                "roles": [],
                "title": null
              },
              {
                "blocks": [
                  {
                    "context": "paragraph",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "Spring control flow pointcuts are conceptually similar to AspectJ "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "cflow"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " pointcuts, although less powerful. (There is currently no way to specify that a pointcut runs below a join point matched by another pointcut.) A control flow pointcut matches the current call stack. For example, it might fire if the join point was invoked by a method in the "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "com.mycompany.web"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " package or by the "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "SomeCaller"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " class. Control flow pointcuts are specified by using the "
                      },
                      {
                        "id": null,
                        "inlines": [
                          {
                            "kind": "text",
                            "value": "org.springframework.aop.support.ControlFlowPointcut"
                          }
                        ],
                        "kind": "monospace",
                        "roles": []
                      },
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": " class."
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null
                  },
                  {
                    "context": "admonition",
                    "id": null,
                    "inlines": [
                      {
                        "kind": "text",
                        "value": "Control flow pointcuts are significantly more expensive to evaluate at runtime than even other dynamic pointcuts. In Java 1.4, the cost is about five times that of other dynamic pointcuts."
                      }
                    ],
                    "roles": [],
                    "title": null,
                    "type": "note"
                  }
                ],
                "context": "section",
                "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-cflow",
                "level": 3,
                "roles": [],
                "title": [
                  {
                    "kind": "text",
                    "value": "Control Flow Pointcuts"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "context": "section",
            "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-dynamic",
            "level": 2,
            "roles": [],
            "title": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Dynamic pointcuts"
              }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "context": "section",
        "id": "aop-api-pointcuts-impls",
        "level": 1,
        "roles": [],
        "title": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Convenience Pointcut Implementations"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "context": "paragraph",
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Spring provides useful pointcut superclasses to help you to implement your own pointcuts."
              }
            ],
            "roles": [],
            "title": null
          },
